b=[qa('Should an ambitious engineer focus on work or explicitly target promotions?', ['Cantrill offers a third path: first understand why the desired title matters. A distinguished-engineer label is an external marker; attaining it leaves the person facing the same question about meaning. He would help a mentee identify work that is important to them and supports continuing development.', 'He points to unhappy senior engineers and wealthy people to challenge the assumption that achievement necessarily supplies fulfillment. His provocative counseling jokes are condensed into that underlying concern, rather than presented as psychological diagnoses. He thinks meaningful motivation provides the fuel from which work and promotion can follow.'],0,140.16,[follow('What if the actual goal is financial independence?', 'He asks what independence enables. If it means never needing to work for money, what would the person then choose to do? He wants that motivating activity identified now rather than assuming money itself answers the question.',141.24,194.12),follow('What if the meaningful activity cannot support a living?', ['Then the person must balance it with a sustainable career. He recognizes the need to provide for oneself and a family, but objects to postponing all meaningful activity until an uncertain future financial milestone.', 'He recalls dot-com wealth existing on paper and then disappearing. The bust forced many people to rediscover why they were in technology apart from money. Some people who sold successfully faced a different problem: they no longer needed to work but still had to find purpose. Buying more houses or consuming more did not automatically answer it.'],195.76,383.92)]),qa('Why do some people reach financial independence and then feel lost?', 'A singular goal can organize life so completely that completion removes the structure. Cantrill says this can happen even after a meaningful engineering achievement: he anticipated a letdown after Oxide shipped its first rack because people had pulled toward that goal for so long. A financial milestone may create the same gap without having supplied meaningful work along the way. He does not endorse responding merely by escalating the amount one wants.',388.72,474),qa('What about someone who would be happy simply relaxing?', ['The host offers a real counterexample to the work-centered view. Cantrill responds with a story of an early Yahoo employee whose fortunate equity position made him wealthy despite limited interest in work. The employee’s responsibilities eventually narrowed considerably, and he still complained about the job.', 'The anecdote illustrates that unusual financial luck and dislike of work can coexist. Cantrill hopes the person found meaning elsewhere, but does not know that outcome. He does not actually establish that nobody can be content with leisure, so the host’s broader counterexample remains unresolved.'],474.8,617.64)]
save('source-0b19b01a734b60d2','Ask what the title is supposed to give you','Bryan Cantrill argues that promotion and financial goals need an underlying source of meaning, including a plan for life after success.',b,'career',[],'The complete single chunk was read. All substantive questions and ordered follow-ups retained. Final leisure counterexample is not resolved by the guest’s anecdote and remains explicitly open. Normal overlapping clip receives its own source section.')
